We’d wrap the call in a try/catch. `ParkingLot.enterVehicle` would catch the specific `NoSpotAvailableException` (or a generic `RuntimeException` if we don’t define one) and then build the same failure `TicketResult` the null‑path currently creates. In code it’s basically:

```java
public TicketResult enterVehicle(Vehicle v) {
    try {
        Spot spot = allocationStrategy.allocate(v, floors);
        // … normal success flow …
    } catch (NoSpotAvailableException e) {
        return TicketResult.failure("No suitable spot");
    }
}
```

So the external contract stays unchanged – the caller still receives a `TicketResult` with `success = false` and an error message, no exception propagates out.
